 ### Format conversion for Python users
 
-If collaborators use the Python/Scanpy ecosystem, you can convert your Seurat
-object to AnnData format using the `sceasy` package:
+If collaborators use the Python/Scanpy ecosystem, you can write your Seurat
+object as an AnnData `.h5ad` file with the Bioconductor package `anndataR`.
+It writes the file directly from R, so no Python installation is needed:
 
 ```{r convert-anndata, eval=FALSE}
-# Install sceasy if needed: remotes::install_github("cellgeni/sceasy")
-library(sceasy)
-sceasy::convertFormat(pbmc,
-                      from = "seurat",
-                      to = "anndata",
-                      outFile = "pbmc_annotated.h5ad")
+# Install anndataR if needed: BiocManager::install("anndataR")
+library(anndataR)
+write_h5ad(pbmc, "pbmc_annotated.h5ad")
 ```
 
+The counts, normalized data, cell metadata and the PCA and UMAP embeddings
+are all carried over. Warnings that matrix column names "cannot be written"
+are expected: AnnData stores embeddings without column names.
+
+::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::: callout
+
+## Why not sceasy?
+
+Older tutorials use `sceasy::convertFormat()`. Its latest version (0.0.7)
+calls `GetAssayData(slot = ...)`, which SeuratObject 5 no longer accepts, so
+it stops with an error on Seurat v5 objects.
+
